/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch06_adaptive_runaway  --  the batch02_adiabatic esterification integrated
  with ADAPTIVE Rosenbrock23 time-stepping, the step history printed glass-box
  so the student sees the controller at work.  (The directory name keeps
  "runaway" from the old exotherm; there is no runaway any more.)

  The physics below is inherited from batch02_adiabatic, unchanged:
  pseudo-first-order esterification (AcOH + EtOH -> EtOAc + H2O) in an
  adiabatic batch reactor.

    * T_0 = 320 K (k_0 ~ 3.7e-4 s^-1, tau_0 ~ 2700 s).
    * dH_rxn = -2.43 kJ/mol of ethanol at 320 K, computed from the species'
      standardThermochemistry (elements datum; the engine's own
      `[reaction heat]` line).  It is about ten times smaller than the
      -26.06 kJ/mol this case used to print: the acetic-acid correction
      (docs/design/acetic-acid-latent-heat-closure.md) made the liquid
      esterification nearly thermoneutral.
    * Equimolar ethanol + acetic acid charge; ethyl acetate + water are
      the products.  Mass-conserving, so the energy balance is clean.
    * Result: T rises only 320 -> 322.2 K in the 600 s window, and the
      reaction is only ~22 % complete at the end (it used to reach 428 K
      and complete conversion).  There is no runaway and no plateau; the
      rate barely accelerates because T barely moves.
    * The adiabatic ODE conserves a frozen-dH surrogate (dH_rxn pinned at the
      charge T), which the run announces.

  The original teaching point of this case (thermal runaway: induction, then
  a sharp temperature rise, then a plateau) needs a genuinely exothermic
  reaction; a replacement case on such a reaction is planned.  No parameter
  was changed to recover the runaway.

  The energy balance is just:

       dT/dt = - r * V * dH_rxn / (n_A * Cp_A + n_B * Cp_B)

  Constant-Cp here (polynomial degree 0 for both ethanol and water in
  the database), so the result extrapolates linearly above the nominal
  liquidHeatCapacity Trange (280-373 K) without numerical pathology.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        batchReactor;

        operation
        {
            mode         adiabatic;        // no T_setpoint --- T evolves with E-balance
        }

        reaction    esterification_etac_exo;  // see constant/reactions
    }
);
